  <data key="d21">Experiment</data>
  <data key="d22">Operando Raman spectroscopy</data>
  <data key="d26">raman</data>
  <data key="d27">spectroscopy</data>
  <data key="d28">Raman spectroscopy</data>
  <data key="d29">operando Raman spectroscopic investigation</data>
  <data key="d30">[]</data>
  <data key="d31">{}</data>
  <data key="d23">Used to investigate surface structural changes during operation.</data>
  <data key="d24">["exp_operando_raman"]</data>
  <data key="d25">1</data>
</node>
<node id="obs_steady_dual_sites">
  <data key="d21">ObservationClaim</data>
  <data key="d22">Ru/Ni3V-LDH exhibits steady dual reactive sites, with Ru and Ni sites serving individual catalytic roles in water splitting.</data>
  <data key="d32">structural_observation</data>
  <data key="d33">Ru/Ni3V-LDH exhibits steady dual reactive sites, with Ru and Ni sites serving individual catalytic roles in water splitting.</data>
  <data key="d34">experimental</data>
  <data key="d23">The abstract reports a reconstruction-free bifunctional catalyst with Ru and Ni reactive sites.</data>
  <data key="d24">["obs_steady_dual_sites"]</data>
  <data key="d25">1</data>
</node>
<node id="obs_reconstruction_free_surface">
  <data key="d21">ObservationClaim</data>
  <data key="d22">The presence of atomic Ru on nickel–vanadium LDH is associated with stabilization of the dangling-bond-rich surface and a reconstruction-free surface.</data>
  <data key="d32">structural_observation</data>
  <data key="d33">The presence of atomic Ru on nickel–vanadium LDH is associated with stabilization of the dangling-bond-rich surface and a reconstruction-free surface.</data>
  <data key="d34">experimental</data>
  <data key="d23">This observation is based on in situ XAS and operando Raman investigations.</data>
  <data key="d24">["obs_reconstruction_free_surface"]</data>
  <data key="d25">1</data>
</node>
<node id="claim_msi_stabilizes_ru">
  <data key="d21">MechanismClaim</data>
  <data key="d22">Strong metal–support interaction provided by nickel–vanadium LDH stabilizes the reactive atomic Ru site, producing only a small fluctuation in Ru oxidation state toward cathodic HER without reconstruction.</data>
  <data key="d32">site_stabilization</data>
  <data key="d33">Strong metal–support interaction provided by nickel–vanadium LDH stabilizes the reactive atomic Ru site, producing only a small fluctuation in Ru oxidation state toward cathodic HER without reconstruction.</data>
  <data key="d34">mixed</data>
  <data key="d23">The abstract presents this as the role of the atomic metal–support interaction in maintaining the Ru site during HER.</data>
  <data key="d24">["claim_msi_stabilizes_ru"]</data>
  <data key="d25">1</data>
</node>
<node id="claim_ru_stabilizes_ni">
  <data key="d21">MechanismClaim</data>
  <data key="d22">The atomic Ru site stabilizes the Ni site, giving it greater structural tolerance toward bond constriction and structural distortion during Ni oxidation in anodic OER and promoting an increase in Ni oxidation state that contributes to OER performance.</data>
  <data key="d32">dual_site_bifunctionality</data>
  <data key="d33">The atomic Ru site stabilizes the Ni site, giving it greater structural tolerance toward bond constriction and structural distortion during Ni oxidation in anodic OER and promoting an increase in Ni oxidation state that contributes to OER performance.</data>
  <data key="d34">mixed</data>
  <data key="d23">The abstract assigns complementary stabilization and catalytic roles to Ru and Ni sites.</data>
  <data key="d24">["claim_ru_stabilizes_ni"]</data>
  <data key="d25">1</data>
</node>
